# mem_accum_return.tkr — the detector that the self-append predicate does NOT over-classify a slice
# that ESCAPES via return (0.3.1 transicao-move-on-return-e-seletor-n-niveis §1b, port 2/4).
#
# WHAT IT PINS. `build` grows `xs` by self-appends and returns it. The return is an escaping position,
# so `mark_stmt` marks `xs` escaping and `binding_is_block_local` (even with the A2 self-append base
# carve-out) returns false — the accumulator stays a `Caller`-tier value that outlives the callee's
# block, never routed to a block region. The A2 change admits the self-append BASE read as an inside
# read ONLY when the WHOLE read equation balances; a tail/return read tips it back to escaping, so the
# accumulator is not wrongly proven block-local.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES. `run` captures the returned slice, then `churn` recycles ten scopes' worth of region
# memory, and finally fingerprints `xs[0] == 0` and `xs[n-1] == 3*(n-1)`. `15` (1 + 4 buffer weight +
# 10 churn hits) witnesses the returned buffer survived the callee's exit and the churn — a slice
# wrongly proven block-local would have died at `build`'s block exit and read back corrupt.
#
# C ROUTE by construction: `Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]` pins the shared build to the C backend.

Feature: mem_accum_return — a returned self-append accumulator escapes its frame and is not freed early

  Scenario: a returned accumulator fingerprints to 15 after the caller's recycling churn
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "15"
